// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Adapted from Goblint tests/regression/34-localwn_restart/02-hybrid.c
// (Amato-Scozzari, SAS 2013, after Halbwachs-Henry, SAS 2012). Goblint
// proves 0 <= i <= 10 inside the inner loop only with
// solvers.td3.restart.wpoint.enabled (restart.wpoint.once disabled), or
// under the sl4 solver: the inner widening point must be restarted from
// bottom once the outer head narrows.
//
// Voblint answers !(10 < i) UNKNOWN. Mechanism: the inner head is its
// own widening point, and i flows around the inner back-edge unrefined
// (the inner guard j < 10 says nothing about i). On the outer head's
// first widening, i := i + 1 delivers [1,+inf] to the inner head; the
// outer head later narrows to [0,9] and delivers [1,10], but the inner
// head's right-hand side joins that with its own back-edge value
// [1,+inf], so [1,+inf] is a self-fixpoint that narrowing never
// re-descends (measured: i = [1,+inf] at both checks). Removing the
// inner loop -- the same program with the checks directly in the outer
// body -- measures i = [1,10] and PROVED x2, so the loss is the inner
// widening point, not the outer narrowing.
// Solver-invariant across join, per-origin, warrow, warrow-per-origin.
void main() {
  i := 0;
  while (true) {
    i := i + 1;
    j := 0;
    while (j < 10) {
      __voblint_check(!(i < 0)); // PROVED
      __voblint_check(!(10 < i)); // UNKNOWN
      j := j + 1
    };
    if (9 < i) {
      i := 0
    } else {
      skip
    }
  }
}
